SQL is the standard language for relational databases: DDL statements such as CREATE TABLE define schema, while DML statements such as SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE and DELETE manipulate data. A join combines rows from two tables on a related column. INNER JOIN returns only matching rows, LEFT JOIN returns all rows from the left table with NULLs where there is no match, and the WHERE and GROUP BY clauses filter and aggregate rows.

Formula: SELECT columns FROM A INNER JOIN B ON A.key = B.key; LEFT JOIN keeps every row of A. Aggregate: SELECT dept, COUNT(*) FROM Employee GROUP BY dept HAVING COUNT(*) > 5;

Worked example: Students: (1, Ana), (2, Ben), (3, Cara). Enrollments: (1, CS101), (1, MA101), (2, CS101). SELECT name FROM Students INNER JOIN Enrollments ON Students.id = Enrollments.student_id returns 3 rows: Ana, Ana, Ben. Cara has no enrollment, so she is excluded; a LEFT JOIN would return 4 rows with NULL for Cara.

A set of vectors is linearly independent when the only way to combine them to get the zero vector is to use all zero coefficients; otherwise the set is linearly dependent, meaning at least one vector is a combination of the others. The span of a set is all vectors obtainable as linear combinations of it. A basis is a linearly independent set that spans the whole space, and every basis of R^n has exactly n vectors.

Formula: c1*v1 + c2*v2 + ... + ck*vk = 0 implies c1 = c2 = ... = ck = 0 (independence); span(v1, ..., vk) = {a1*v1 + ... + ak*vk}

Worked example: Vectors (1, 2) and (2, 4) are linearly dependent because (2, 4) = 2 * (1, 2), so 2*(1, 2) - 1*(2, 4) = (0, 0) with non-zero coefficients. In contrast, (1, 0) and (0, 1) are independent: c1*(1, 0) + c2*(0, 1) = (0, 0) forces c1 = 0 and c2 = 0.

A linked list is a chain of nodes, each holding data and a pointer to the next node (and, in a doubly linked list, to the previous node as well). Unlike arrays, nodes need not be contiguous, so inserting or deleting at a known position costs O(1) by rewiring pointers. The trade-off is that reaching the i-th element requires walking from the head, so access and search cost O(n), and each node carries extra pointer memory.

Formula: Access/search: O(n); insert/delete at head or at a known node: O(1); insert/delete at position i: O(n) to reach it, then O(1) to rewire; node = {data, next}

Worked example: List 10 -> 20 -> 30. To insert 15 after 10: create node 15, set its next pointer to 20, then set 10's next pointer to 15, giving 10 -> 15 -> 20 -> 30. No elements are shifted; only two pointers change. Reaching the 3rd element still needs 3 steps from the head.

A linear ODE of order n with constant coefficients is solved by its characteristic equation: distinct real roots give exponentials, repeated roots give exponentials times polynomials, and complex roots give exponentially modulated sines and cosines. The general solution is the complementary function plus a particular integral, found by undetermined coefficients or variation of parameters. Existence and uniqueness hold when the coefficients and forcing term are continuous.

Formula: For a y'' + b y' + c y = 0, characteristic a r^2 + b r + c = 0; roots r1 != r2 give y = C1 e^{r1 x} + C2 e^{r2 x}; repeated root r gives (C1 + C2 x) e^{r x}

Worked example: y'' - 3y' + 2y = 0 has characteristic r^2 - 3r + 2 = (r-1)(r-2), so y = C1 e^x + C2 e^{2x}. For y'' + y = 0 the roots are +-i, giving y = C1 cos x + C2 sin x.

Worked example: y'' - 3y' + 2y = 0 has characteristic r^2 - 3r + 2 = (r-1)(r-2), so y = C1 e^x + C2 e^{2x}. For y'' + y = 0 the roots are +-i, giving y = C1 cos x + C2 sin x.

Formula: For a y'' + b y' + c y = 0, characteristic a r^2 + b r + c = 0; roots r1 != r2 give y = C1 e^{r1 x} + C2 e^{r2 x}; repeated root r gives (C1 + C2 x) e^{r x}

A linear ODE of order n with constant coefficients is solved by its characteristic equation: distinct real roots give exponentials, repeated roots give exponentials times polynomials, and complex roots give exponentially modulated sines and cosines. The general solution is the complementary function plus a particular integral, found by undetermined coefficients or variation of parameters. Existence and uniqueness hold when the coefficients and forcing term are continuous.

The continuous uniform(a,b) spreads mass evenly over an interval; the exponential(lambda) models waiting times and is memoryless; the gamma(alpha, beta) generalizes the exponential to the waiting time for alpha events; the beta(a,b) lives on (0,1) and models proportions; the Weibull models lifetimes with a changing hazard rate; the normal(mu, sigma^2) is the bell curve that the central limit theorem produces. Many of these are special cases of each other.

Formula: Exponential pdf: lambda e^{-lambda x}, mean 1/lambda, memoryless P(X > s+t | X > s) = P(X > t); Gamma mean = alpha/beta; Beta mean = a/(a+b); Weibull pdf: (k/lambda)(x/lambda)^(k-1) e^{-(x/lambda)^k}

Worked example: If buses arrive as a Poisson process at 6 per hour, the waiting time is Exponential(6 per hour) with mean 10 minutes. The sum of two independent Exponential(lambda) variables is Gamma(2, lambda). A proportion with a Beta(2,2) prior is symmetric around 0.5.

Worked example: If buses arrive as a Poisson process at 6 per hour, the waiting time is Exponential(6 per hour) with mean 10 minutes. The sum of two independent Exponential(lambda) variables is Gamma(2, lambda). A proportion with a Beta(2,2) prior is symmetric around 0.5.

Formula: Exponential pdf: lambda e^{-lambda x}, mean 1/lambda, memoryless P(X > s+t | X > s) = P(X > t); Gamma mean = alpha/beta; Beta mean = a/(a+b); Weibull pdf: (k/lambda)(x/lambda)^(k-1) e^{-(x/lambda)^k}

The continuous uniform(a,b) spreads mass evenly over an interval; the exponential(lambda) models waiting times and is memoryless; the gamma(alpha, beta) generalizes the exponential to the waiting time for alpha events; the beta(a,b) lives on (0,1) and models proportions; the Weibull models lifetimes with a changing hazard rate; the normal(mu, sigma^2) is the bell curve that the central limit theorem produces. Many of these are special cases of each other.
